---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lab - Steve Wang"
  description="Smaller automation from Steve Wang's purchasing and sales reporting work — a weekly sales reporting app, lapsed-customer reports, promotion impact reports, and spreadsheet extraction scripts."
  current="lab"
>
  <main>
    <section class="page-title section-wrap">
      <h1>Lab</h1>
      <p>
        Smaller pieces of automation from day-to-day purchasing and sales
        reporting work — not flagship projects. These run privately on
        internal data, so they are described here rather than hosted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-wrap project-list">
      <article class="detail-card" id="weekly-sales-reporting">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Reporting app</span>
            <h2>Weekly Sales Reporting App</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · now part of Ordering Dashboard</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Originally a standalone desktop app for the weekly sales cycle. Excel/ODS exports are
          dragged in, auto-detected for type, week, and region, and checked
          against the existing history with anomaly warnings before anything is
          written — every import takes an automatic backup first, so a bad file
          is one click to undo. A dashboard tab summarises KPIs, top movers, and
          stock alerts, each table carries a week-over-week sparkline, and
          formatted reports export back out with the highlighting rules already
          applied.
        </p>
        <p>
          It started as a plain Python importer loading weekly exports into a
          local SQLite database — a zero-install local warehouse for a machine
          that allows no admin rights. Once that history was worth querying, the
          reporting around it was still being redone by hand every week, so the
          same headless core picked up a small local interface and kept the
          import/export logic independent of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Purchasing and weekly reporting were reading the same sales exports
          twice, so the app was folded into the
          <a href="/projects/#purchasing-workflow-tools">Ordering Dashboard</a>
          as its Weekly Report tab. It is no longer maintained separately: one
          import now serves both workflows, over one database.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card" id="lapsed-customer-reports">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Sales analysis</span>
            <h2>Lapsed-Customer Reports</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · on request</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Finds customers who stopped ordering given items — quantities are
          netted per invoice so fully returned orders don't count as
          purchases, with per-item or grouped output and "weeks since last
          order" thresholds to separate lapsed from active.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sales teams kept asking "who used to buy this and stopped?"; this
          turns a raw sales export into a formatted chase list in seconds
          instead of manual pivot work.
        </p>
        <details class="lab-demo">
          <summary>Demo with sample data</summary>
          <div class="lab-demo-body">
            <p class="tool-note">
              Fabricated invoice lines for seven fictional venues and two
              items, measured against the most recent invoice date in the
              sample (26 Jun 2026) — exactly how the real script works.
            </p>
            <div class="demo-controls">
              <div class="tool-field">
                <label for="lapsed-weeks">Flag as lapsed after (weeks)</label>
                <input id="lapsed-weeks" type="number" inputmode="numeric" min="1" step="1" value="4" />
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="demo-count" id="lapsed-count" aria-live="polite">—</p>
            <div class="demo-table-wrap">
              <table class="demo-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Customer</th>
                    <th>Item</th>
                    <th>Last buy</th>
                    <th>Weeks since</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody id="lapsed-rows"></tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="tool-note">
              † This venue's most recent invoice was fully credited, so it
              doesn't count as a purchase — the last buy falls back to the
              earlier invoice. Returns are netted per invoice, exactly as in
              the real report.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card" id="post-promotion-report">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Reporting automation</span>
            <h2>Post-Promotion Impact Report</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · monthly</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Cross-references monthly promotion item lists against the shared
          SQLite sales database to report which customers actually bought the
          promoted items, delivered as a formatted Excel workbook.
        </p>
        <p>
          Monthly promotions needed a follow-up answer to "did it move
          product, and with whom?" without re-opening a stack of weekly
          spreadsheets.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card" id="shipment-extractor">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Data extraction</span>
            <h2>Messy-Spreadsheet Shipment Extractor</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · on request</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Parses supplier stock workbooks that were formatted for humans —
          regex detection of shipment dates and quantities across
          inconsistent sheets and merged headers — into one clean
          incoming-shipments table.
        </p>
        <p>
          Supplier files arrive in a different layout every time; this
          replaces re-reading a dozen sheets by eye with one CSV.
        </p>
        <details class="lab-demo">
          <summary>Demo with sample data</summary>
          <div class="lab-demo-body">
            <p class="tool-note">
              A fabricated example of what arrives versus what the script
              extracts. Shipment rows hide inside free-text headers in two
              different date styles.
            </p>
            <span class="project-meta">Supplier file — as received</span>
            <div class="demo-table-wrap">
              <table class="demo-table">
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread" colspan="3"><strong>ECO PACKAGING PTY — STOCK &amp; SHIPMENTS</strong></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>4501</td>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread">Coffee Cup 8oz (1000/ctn)</td>
                    <td>SOH 320</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>4502</td>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread">Coffee Cup Lid 8oz (2000/ctn)</td>
                    <td>SOH #N/A</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread" colspan="3">SEA SHIPMENT — ETA 15/08/2026</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>4501</td>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread"></td>
                    <td>600</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread" colspan="3">NEXT ORDER ETD SEPT 3</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>4502</td>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread"></td>
                    <td>400</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <span class="project-meta">Extracted result</span>
            <div class="demo-table-wrap">
              <table class="demo-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Item</th>
                    <th>Description</th>
                    <th>Type</th>
                    <th>Date</th>
                    <th>Qty</th>
                    <th>Sheet</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>4501</td>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread">Coffee Cup 8oz (1000/ctn)</td>
                    <td>ETA</td>
                    <td>15/08/2026</td>
                    <td>600</td>
                    <td>QLD</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>4502</td>
                    <td class="demo-cell-spread">Coffee Cup Lid 8oz (2000/ctn)</td>
                    <td>ETD</td>
                    <td>03/09/2026</td>
                    <td>400</td>
                    <td>QLD</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="tool-note">
              The real script walks a folder of these workbooks, regex-matching
              both <code>15/08/2026</code>-style dates and <code>SEPT 3</code>-style
              month names in the header rows, and attributes each quantity to
              the right item and sheet.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  interface InvoiceLine {
    customer: string;
    item: string;
    invoice: string;
    date: string;
    qty: number;
  }

  // Fabricated demo data — fictional venues, items, and invoice numbers.
  const LINES: InvoiceLine[] = [
    { customer: "Golden Wok Noodle Bar", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1001", date: "2026-03-06", qty: 4 },
    { customer: "Golden Wok Noodle Bar", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1102", date: "2026-04-10", qty: 4 },
    { customer: "Golden Wok Noodle Bar", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1230", date: "2026-05-15", qty: 6 },
    { customer: "Golden Wok Noodle Bar", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1355", date: "2026-06-19", qty: 4 },
    { customer: "Golden Wok Noodle Bar", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1230", date: "2026-05-15", qty: 2 },
    { customer: "Golden Wok Noodle Bar", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1355", date: "2026-06-19", qty: 2 },
    { customer: "Harbour Lane Café", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-0870", date: "2026-02-20", qty: 2 },
    { customer: "Harbour Lane Café", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1050", date: "2026-03-27", qty: 2 },
    { customer: "Lucky Garden Takeaway", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1080", date: "2026-04-03", qty: 6 },
    { customer: "Lucky Garden Takeaway", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1190", date: "2026-05-08", qty: 6 },
    { customer: "Lucky Garden Takeaway", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1310", date: "2026-06-12", qty: 6 },
    { customer: "Lucky Garden Takeaway", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1080", date: "2026-04-03", qty: 1 },
    { customer: "Lucky Garden Takeaway", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1190", date: "2026-05-08", qty: 1 },
    { customer: "Miso House", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1260", date: "2026-05-22", qty: 3 },
    { customer: "Miso House", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1400", date: "2026-06-26", qty: 3 },
    { customer: "Miso House", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1150", date: "2026-04-24", qty: 2 },
    { customer: "Miso House", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1352", date: "2026-06-19", qty: 6 },
    { customer: "Miso House", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1352", date: "2026-06-19", qty: -6 },
    { customer: "The Corner Bistro", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1290", date: "2026-06-05", qty: 2 },
    { customer: "Bamboo Express", item: "Chilli Oil 12×500ml", invoice: "INV-1010", date: "2026-03-13", qty: 4 },
    { customer: "Bamboo Express", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1275", date: "2026-05-29", qty: 4 },
    { customer: "Sunny Point Fish & Chips", item: "Soy Sauce 4×1.8L", invoice: "INV-1401", date: "2026-06-26", qty: 2 },
  ];

  const DAY = 86400000;

  function setupLapsedDemo() {
    const weeksInput = document.getElementById("lapsed-weeks") as HTMLInputElement;
    const countEl = document.getElementById("lapsed-count") as HTMLElement;
    const bodyEl = document.getElementById("lapsed-rows") as HTMLElement;

    const asOf = Math.max(...LINES.map((line) => Date.parse(line.date)));

    // Net quantity per customer+item+invoice; only invoices with a net
    // quantity > 0 count as a purchase (a fully credited buy nets to zero).
    const invoices = new Map<string, { customer: string; item: string; date: number; net: number }>();
    for (const line of LINES) {
      const key = `${line.customer}|${line.item}|${line.invoice}`;
      const entry = invoices.get(key);
      if (entry) {
        entry.net += line.qty;
      } else {
        invoices.set(key, { customer: line.customer, item: line.item, date: Date.parse(line.date), net: line.qty });
      }
    }

    const pairs = new Map<string, { customer: string; item: string; lastBuy: number; lastRaw: number }>();
    for (const inv of invoices.values()) {
      const key = `${inv.customer}|${inv.item}`;
      const pair = pairs.get(key) ?? { customer: inv.customer, item: inv.item, lastBuy: -Infinity, lastRaw: -Infinity };
      pair.lastRaw = Math.max(pair.lastRaw, inv.date);
      if (inv.net > 0) pair.lastBuy = Math.max(pair.lastBuy, inv.date);
      pairs.set(key, pair);
    }

    const rows = [...pairs.values()]
      .map((pair) => ({
        ...pair,
        weeks: Math.floor((asOf - pair.lastBuy) / DAY / 7),
        credited: pair.lastRaw > pair.lastBuy,
      }))
      .sort((a, b) => b.weeks - a.weeks || a.customer.localeCompare(b.customer));

    function update() {
      const threshold = Number(weeksInput.value);
      if (!Number.isInteger(threshold) || threshold < 1) {
        countEl.textContent = "—";
        bodyEl.innerHTML = "";
        return;
      }

      const lapsed = rows.filter((row) => row.weeks >= threshold).length;
      countEl.textContent = `${lapsed} of ${rows.length} customer–item pairs lapsed at ${threshold} week${threshold === 1 ? "" : "s"}.`;

      bodyEl.innerHTML = rows
        .map((row) => {
          const isLapsed = row.weeks >= threshold;
          const lastBuy = new Date(row.lastBuy).toLocaleDateString("en-AU", {
            day: "numeric",
            month: "short",
            year: "numeric",
          });
          return `<tr${isLapsed ? ' class="demo-row-lapsed"' : ""}>
            <td>${row.customer}${row.credited ? " †" : ""}</td>
            <td>${row.item}</td>
            <td>${lastBuy}</td>
            <td>${row.weeks} wk${row.weeks === 1 ? "" : "s"}${isLapsed ? '<span class="demo-tag">Lapsed</span>' : ""}</td>
          </tr>`;
        })
        .join("");
    }

    weeksInput.addEventListener("input", update);
    update();
  }

  setupLapsedDemo();
</script>
